 | Harness fixed cost: the per-shard cost outside the entries themselves (worktrees, baseline builds, baseline checks; the harness prints it as "fixed cost", slowest worker). P4-12 narrowed what each entry builds and barely moved it, 516 s to 463 s on GitHub's slowest shard, so another per-entry optimisation is the wrong lever. **The measurement to beat** (run 36819512559, P4-12's tooling, first run): the tightest harness clock was 758 s (12.6 min) of the 900 s ceiling, `5acbd0d` shard 7, of which 464 s was fixed cost. The slowest per-commit job step was 13.8 min (`032a0dd` shard 1), but 2.8 min of it was preflight, build, gates and tests, outside the harness clock. The two designs to choose between when it comes due: (a) one baseline build shared by every worker's worktree, each worktree rebuilding only what its patch touches; (b) a persistent pool of worktrees, built once per shard and reset between entries. First step either way: the harness prints the fixed cost split into worktrees, baseline builds and baseline checks. That split has not been measured, and if baseline checks dominate (the `Raft.Scale.Tests` suite took 301 s on GitHub in the same run), neither design is the lever (reviewer, at P4 acceptance). **Another data point (P5-05, full local run of the phase-5 head):** fixed cost 442-487 s locally in the four shards that hold a whole-suite entry and 92-192 s in the four that do not, against 258 s at P4-12, the difference the baseline run of the whole suite; the third time harness cost has forced a design change, each time at a different layer (P4-11 the project split, P4-12 per-project builds, P5-07 whole-suite entries narrowed to the projects their mechanism needs). **The split, first measured (P5-07, which made the harness print it per worker):** setup 0-5 s; each worker's baseline build 34-59 s; baseline checks of test projects up to 119 s (`Raft.Scale.Tests`, filtered to the shard's targets) and 97 s (`Raft.Budget.Tests`); command baselines 0-24 s, except `scripts/ci-test.sh Raft.Scale.Tests` at 209 s (S-ran-2's). Fixed cost per shard 81-275 s locally; outside command baselines it did not move. Baseline checks of the costliest test projects, not worktrees or builds, are what remains: the case in which the reviewer said neither design (a) nor (b) is the lever. **Phase 6 (P6-01, P6-13):** measured on GitHub (run 37160452711), the queueing longest-first assignment would remove was 3-20 s per shard, so it was not built; S-ran-2's 281 s command baseline was removed by narrowing it (P6-13: 5 s locally). What remains is the `Raft.Scale.Tests` baseline check, 86-131 s per shard on GitHub, which is the cost of running a target's own project once before its entries. No test closes the row; **re-promised to phase 7 at the phase-6 report**, for the reviewer to keep there or drop | P7 | open | — |
 | A structural measure of linearizability-checking cost, so that the spec can state a genuine limit. P5-05 measured over 60,000 keys that no per-key count separates the histories the WGL checker decides from those it cannot: neither indeterminate Appends, nor those observed by a read, nor all indeterminate writes (one undecided key had zero observed indeterminate Appends; the costliest decided key, 865,895 states, had no indeterminate Appends at all). Cost follows how indeterminate writes overlap and interleave with the observations on one key. Candidate to measure, with its own prediction: the widest set of indeterminate writes overlapping in time on one key. Until then the budget is what the enforcing runner holds and a history exceeding it fails the soak (reviewer, P5-05); no count can be the limit **Moved to its own phase, 11, at phase 6's approval (reviewer). The prediction, recorded here before any measurement:** the concurrency width of a key's sub-history, the largest number of operations pending at one instant with every indeterminate operation counted as open to the end of the history, separates the decided keys from the undecided ones, KL-1 included: every undecided key has a width at least 4 above the largest width of any key decided in under 1,000,000 states, because an operation that never responds stays concurrent with everything after it and the search's frontier grows exponentially with the operations concurrent at once. Candidates fixed with it, before the measurement: the number of indeterminate writes overlapping one another in real time, and the number of distinct values an observed read could have seen | P11 | open | — |
